1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,092
Quantum computing attracts big promises.

2
00:00:02,092 --> 00:00:08,502
A better place to begin is smaller: with a state, a
circuit, and a prediction you can check.

3
00:00:08,502 --> 00:00:16,572
Qubit Atlas is a guided field course for learning how
quantum computation actually works, from your first
qubit to experiments you can explain.

4
00:00:16,572 --> 00:00:20,989
Lessons pair clear explanations with worked examples
and experiments you can repeat.

5
00:00:20,989 --> 00:00:22,817
Start with the model.

6
00:00:22,817 --> 00:00:29,129
A qubit is described by complex amplitudes, not by a
tiny classical coin hiding a definite answer.

7
00:00:29,129 --> 00:00:31,647
The squared magnitudes give measurement probabilities.

8
00:00:31,647 --> 00:00:37,614
Relative phase can change what a later gate does, even
when the first measurement looks unchanged.

9
00:00:37,614 --> 00:00:42,891
Normalization keeps total probability at one; relative
phase tells later gates how amplitudes relate.

10
00:00:42,891 --> 00:00:47,702
A circuit applies gates in an order, then measurement
returns a classical sample.

11
00:00:47,702 --> 00:00:51,168
Run the same experiment again and the counts vary.

12
00:00:51,168 --> 00:00:55,643
As shots accumulate, the observed frequencies tend to
approach the predicted distribution.

13
00:00:55,643 --> 00:00:58,099
One run never reveals every amplitude.

14
00:00:58,099 --> 00:01:05,602
At twenty shots, counts can wander; with a thousand,
they tend to sit closer to the prediction. Both remain
finite estimates.

15
00:01:05,602 --> 00:01:09,649
Try Hadamard, Z, Hadamard on a qubit prepared in zero.

16
00:01:09,649 --> 00:01:14,055
The middle gate changes relative phase, not the
immediate Z probabilities.

17
00:01:14,055 --> 00:01:20,251
The final Hadamard turns that phase difference into
interference: the outcome switches from zero to one.

18
00:01:20,251 --> 00:01:23,582
This small experiment makes an abstract idea
observable.

19
00:01:23,582 --> 00:01:28,975
Then follow a complete path: ten connected modules and
thirty worked lessons.

20
00:01:28,975 --> 00:01:33,151
Build foundations in states, amplitudes, measurement,
and linear algebra.

21
00:01:33,151 --> 00:01:36,516
Progress through gates, circuits, entanglement, and
algorithms.

22
00:01:36,516 --> 00:01:43,126
Continue into noise, error correction, hardware, and
the methods for designing and reporting an experiment.

23
00:01:43,126 --> 00:01:48,924
Each lesson pairs a clear objective with a worked
example, experiment, and checkpoint.

24
00:01:48,924 --> 00:01:51,772
In the browser lab, compose gates yourself.

25
00:01:51,772 --> 00:01:59,425
Choose one to four qubits, prepare a circuit, select X,
Y, or Z measurement, and decide how many shots to run.

26
00:01:59,425 --> 00:02:04,676
Inspect the state vector, the ideal prediction, and the
finite sample side by side.

27
00:02:04,676 --> 00:02:08,898
Choose a gate and target, then inspect the model's
predicted outcomes.

28
00:02:08,898 --> 00:02:13,035
Build a Bell pair with a Hadamard and a controlled NOT.

29
00:02:13,035 --> 00:02:18,517
In the ideal Z-basis model, only zero-zero and one-one
appear, each with probability one half.

30
00:02:18,517 --> 00:02:22,318
Each qubit alone is balanced; together, their outcomes
are correlated.

31
00:02:22,318 --> 00:02:27,127
A circuit simulator demonstrates this prediction. It is
not a physical Bell test.

32
00:02:27,127 --> 00:02:32,610
The ideal joint state contains only zero-zero and
one-one, though either qubit alone is random.

33
00:02:32,610 --> 00:02:39,422
From there, study search, phase estimation, and the
quantum Fourier transform with their assumptions in
view.

34
00:02:39,422 --> 00:02:43,871
Learn density matrices, decoherence, and why error
correction needs resources.

35
00:02:43,871 --> 00:02:48,320
Connect abstract circuits to hardware constraints,
compilation, and hybrid workflows.

36
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:55,526
Ask where a speedup comes from, what resources it
costs, and what the ideal model leaves out.

37
00:02:55,526 --> 00:03:01,962
Every experiment starts with a question, a prediction,
a measurement choice, and a record of its limits.

38
00:03:01,962 --> 00:03:04,881
Guided examples and self-checks build the habit.

39
00:03:04,881 --> 00:03:13,075
Use the glossary when notation gets dense, track your
local progress, and export circuits as OpenQASM 2 for
use in other tools.

40
00:03:13,075 --> 00:03:18,807
Record the circuit, basis, shot count, and assumptions
so another learner can interpret the result.

41
00:03:18,807 --> 00:03:23,813
Qubit Atlas runs its ideal state-vector simulator
locally in your browser, up to four qubits.

42
00:03:23,813 --> 00:03:27,590
It does not run on quantum hardware or model device
noise.

43
00:03:27,590 --> 00:03:29,217
Start with lesson one.

44
00:03:29,217 --> 00:03:31,766
Build the model before believing the headline.
